{
  "requirement": "A requirements or definition input MUST be rewritten only if the\n{term}`requirements.refactor.mapped_back` current text of each definition it\nrecords equals the recorded text and, for a requirements input, the mapped\nselection's current matches are exactly the recorded matches with the mapping\napplied.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations from `requirements.refactor.definition_inputs`.** A requirements or definition input is rewritten only if two conditions hold:\n- (a) The mapped-back current text of each recorded definition equals the recorded text.\n- (b) For a requirements input, the mapped selection's current matches are exactly the recorded matches with the mapping applied.\n\n`requirements.refactor.mapped_back` defines the mapped-back form: each new side of the mappings is replaced by its old side.\n\n**Test coverage:**\n- **Text equal, requirements input rewritten.** `tests/refactor.rs#fn[a_moved_requirement_renames_its_references_and_evidence]` moves `rules.load` to `core.load`. It asserts that the recorded input becomes `requirements_input(\"core.load\", [\"core.load\"], LOAD_TEXT)`. The rewrite therefore depends on the text matching after being mapped back.\n- **Text equal, definition input rewritten.** `tests/refactor.rs#fn[a_moved_term_rewrites_definition_inputs_and_the_texts_using_it]` asserts `inputs[1][\"definition\"] == \"glossary.term\"`. It also covers a recorded requirement text that contains the moved name in `{term}`, which must be mapped back to compare equal. The test finishes with `usage_errors` empty.\n- **Text changed, requirements input left stale.** `tests/refactor.rs#fn[a_requirement_edited_while_moving_keeps_its_old_record]` asserts that the dependent `rules.check` record reports `core.load no longer selects what it recorded` and that its bytes are unchanged.\n- **Text changed, definition input left stale.** `tests/refactor.rs#fn[a_term_edited_while_moving_leaves_its_definition_input_stale]` asserts the `inputs[1]: glossary.term no longer selects what it recorded` report and that the file is unchanged.\n- **Matches exactly the mapped recorded matches.** `tests/refactor.rs#fn[a_moved_chapter_rewrites_glob_selections_and_their_matches]` maps `rules` to `core`. It asserts the selection becomes `core.*` and the matches become `[core.check, core.load]`.\n- **Matches no longer equal, stale because a match was lost.** In `a_moved_requirement_renames_its_references_and_evidence`, `rules.*` loses `load`. The test asserts the report line `inputs[0]: rules.* no longer selects what it recorded`.\n- **Matches no longer equal, stale because a match was added.** `tests/refactor.rs#fn[a_moved_chapter_with_a_new_glob_match_leaves_the_record_stale]` adds a new requirement to the moved chapter. It asserts the stale report and that the record is byte-identical.\n- **Mapped-back transformation.** `src/refactor/tests.rs#fn[mapped_back_text_replaces_whole_new_names_and_paths]` and `src/refactor/tests.rs#fn[opening_fences_map_their_short_names_back]` unit-test the replacement. They cover whole-name boundaries and short names in opening fences, including the cases where the fence is left unchanged.\n\nThe reference `rewrite_input` compares the whole recorded input against the mapped-back resolution. A multi-definition glob input is therefore not a separate code path, because one comparison covers the combined recorded text. I found no distinct scenario that the tests would accept as a violation. All the relevant tests carry `{test}` references to the subject. No acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "31c98ff1e759a45f9a65d38ca57943178396c74bb5b26c157c76de4cb09d4d1b",
